lds%5D"},"trials":{"total":0,"matched_loosely":0,"studies":[],"source":"ClinicalTrials.gov","search_url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/search?intr=Hexyl%20butyrate"},"is_terpenoid":false,"consumer_summary":{"model":"gemini-3.1-pro-preview","generated":"2026-09-11","what_it_is":"Hexyl butyrate is a fatty acid ester formed by the combination of hexanol and butyric acid. Known for its pleasant apple and citrus aroma, it acts as a natural animal metabolite and is widely used as a fragrance ingredient. You likely encounter it in scented products or when enjoying the natural aromas of certain fruits and herbs.","found_in":"This compound is naturally found in a variety of familiar plants, including lavender, apricots, and citrus fruits. It is also a major component of the essential oil of Golpar (Heracleum persicum), a spice commonly used in Persian cuisine.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are particularly drawn to hexyl butyrate for its fascinating role in insect communication, frequently studying it as a pheromone and sex attractant for various bug species. Beyond entomology, scientists are exploring its potential biological effects in humans, with the strongest evidence currently resting at the human observational level (Oxford level 2b). There is also ongoing interest in its safety profile and enzymatic synthesis for use in the fragrance and flavor industries.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"May support the body's natural defenses against certain microbes.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Based on four papers, including human observational research.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":1}],"paper_count":4},{"claim_id":"pain","statement":"Early work points toward a potential role in supportin